Subject: wasix: answer a poll with a zero clock timeout at once
Last-Update: 2026-09-30

poll_oneoff took a clock subscription whose timeout was zero as a wait
with no deadline. WASI defines it as the opposite, a check of what is
ready now, and wasix-libc sends one nanosecond instead of zero to get
that behaviour. A program using the WASI meaning, such as the Go
runtime's scheduler, which polls its sockets with a zero timeout between
runnable goroutines, blocked until an unrelated descriptor became ready.
Zero and one nanosecond now both mean an immediate check: the call
reports what is ready, or the clock subscription when nothing is, as a
timed-out wait does.

diff --git a/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/wasi/poll_oneoff.rs b/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/wasi/poll_oneoff.rs
index 73a96d6..40b31d7 100644
--- a/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/wasi/poll_oneoff.rs
+++ b/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/wasi/poll_oneoff.rs
@@ -290,11 +290,12 @@ where
                         continue;
                     }
 
-                    // If the timeout duration is zero then this is an immediate check rather than
-                    // a sleep itself
-                    if clock_info.timeout == 0 {
-                        time_to_sleep = Duration::MAX;
-                    } else if clock_info.timeout == 1 {
+                    // A timeout of zero, as WASI defines it, or of one
+                    // nanosecond, which wasix-libc sends for a zero poll
+                    // timeout, is an immediate check rather than a sleep:
+                    // the call reports what is ready now, or the clock
+                    // subscription when nothing is.
+                    if clock_info.timeout <= 1 {
                         time_to_sleep = Duration::ZERO;
                         clock_subs.push((clock_info, s.userdata));
                     } else {
